UPDATE destination_months SET prose_payoff = $cst$October Yamunotri is farewell and ceremony. The temple closing — the deity carried in procession from the temple to Kharsali, with priests chanting, devotees following, and the mountain watching — is one of Hinduism's most poignant rituals. The admission that winter is beyond human control, the trust that the deity will return in spring, the village accepting six months of dormancy. The closing ceremony draws devoted pilgrims who understand this is the end, not the beginning, and find meaning in it. The autumn colours along the gorge are beautiful. The hot springs in October cold (0-10°C) steam dramatically. The last trek of the season has a bittersweet quality. Hotels at around ₹2,250. If you want to understand Yamunotri beyond the Char Dham checklist, come for the closing.$cst$::text WHERE destination_id||'/'||month = 'yamunotri/10';
UPDATE destination_months SET prose_payoff = $cst$Late September Yamunotri is the reward for patience. The monsoon retreats, the trail dries, and the gorge in autumn light is beautiful — the Yamuna settling to clear flow, the rock faces catching warm light, the surrounding forest holding monsoon green while the sky clears blue. The hot springs in cool, clear air produce their most photogenic steam columns. The temple in September has its last active-season energy. The trek (6km) in September conditions is at its most pleasant: cool air, dry trail, fewer pilgrims. The Saptrishi Kund trek to the Yamuna's actual source is most viable in late September — weather stable, trail dry. Hotels at around ₹2,250 with easy availability. September Yamunotri: the connoisseur's Char Dham.$cst$::text WHERE destination_id||'/'||month = 'yamunotri/9';
UPDATE destination_months SET prose_payoff = $cst$November sangam is when photographers make the trip. The Bhagirathi runs emerald, the Alaknanda runs jade, and the boundary between them — visible from the suspension bridge, from the ghats, from the Raghunath temple terrace — is clean enough to photograph without filters. The water is achingly cold for ritual dips, but the handful of devotees who come dip regardless. The town's dharamshala chai, served in glass tumblers at the ghat edge, warms you while the river view does everything else. The drive from Rishikesh is fog-free and stunning in autumn light. Rooms: around ₹1,400/night.$cst$::text WHERE destination_id||'/'||month = 'devprayag/11';
COMMIT;
